1
00:00:00,851 --> 00:00:14,358
[SPEAKER_00]: The sutta today, it's Anguttara 321, it's called Purity, and it's a short sutta, but quite interesting, useful as well.

2
00:00:15,633 --> 00:00:22,257
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it starts by the Buddha addressing the bhikkhus saying, bhikkhus, there are these three kinds of purity.

3
00:00:22,437 --> 00:00:22,957
[SPEAKER_00]: What three?

4
00:00:23,858 --> 00:00:27,800
[SPEAKER_00]: Purity of body, purity of speech and purity of thought.

5
00:00:29,061 --> 00:00:30,382
[SPEAKER_00]: What is purity of body?

6
00:00:31,343 --> 00:00:36,105
[SPEAKER_00]: It is when a bhikkhu abstains from killing living creatures, stealing and in celibacy.

7
00:00:36,626 --> 00:00:38,387
[SPEAKER_00]: That is called purity of body.

8
00:00:39,713 --> 00:00:40,913
[SPEAKER_00]: What is purity of speech?

9
00:00:41,413 --> 00:00:46,434
[SPEAKER_00]: It is when a bhikkhu abstains from false, malicious, harsh and trivial speech.

10
00:00:47,394 --> 00:00:48,995
[SPEAKER_00]: This is called purity of speech.

11
00:00:50,435 --> 00:00:52,515
[SPEAKER_00]: And what is purity of thought?

12
00:00:53,576 --> 00:01:00,377
[SPEAKER_00]: It is when a bhikkhu, when there is a sensual desire internally, understands, there is a sensual desire internally for me.

13
00:01:01,297 --> 00:01:06,558
[SPEAKER_00]: When there is no sensual desire internally, he understands, there is no sensual desire internally for me.

14
00:01:07,993 --> 00:01:18,760
[SPEAKER_00]: He understands how unarisen sensual desire arises, and how arisen sensual desire is abandoned, and how abandoned sensual desire does not arise again in the future.

15
00:01:20,701 --> 00:01:26,705
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when there is ill-will internally, he understands there is ill-will internally, for me.

16
00:01:27,305 --> 00:01:31,208
[SPEAKER_00]: When there is no ill-will internally, he understands there is no ill-will internally for me.

17
00:01:32,006 --> 00:01:38,329
[SPEAKER_00]: He understands how un-arisen ill-will arises, and how arisen ill-will is abandoned, and how abandoned ill-will does not arise again.

18
00:01:40,130 --> 00:01:46,413
[SPEAKER_00]: When there is indolence and lethargy internally, he understands there is indolence and lethargy internally for me.

19
00:01:46,573 --> 00:01:52,415
[SPEAKER_00]: When there is no indolence and lethargy internally, he understands there is no indolence and lethargy internally for me.

20
00:01:53,256 --> 00:01:59,999
[SPEAKER_00]: He understands how un-arisen indolence and lethargy arises, and how arisen one is abandoned, and how abandoned one does not arise in the future.

21
00:02:02,206 --> 00:02:10,694
[SPEAKER_00]: The fourth hindrance, eh... when there is restlessness and anxiety internally, he understands there is restlessness and anxiety internally for me.

22
00:02:10,794 --> 00:02:21,664
[SPEAKER_00]: When there is no restlessness and anxiety, he understands that there is no restlessness and anxiety and he understands how un-arisen arises and how arisen is abandoned and how abandoned does not arising in the future.

23
00:02:23,105 --> 00:02:24,226
[SPEAKER_00]: I forgot to do this part.

24
00:02:26,761 --> 00:02:30,905
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when there is doubt internally, he understands there is doubt internally for me.

25
00:02:31,445 --> 00:02:34,388
[SPEAKER_00]: When there is no doubt internally, he understands there is no doubt internally for me.

26
00:02:35,429 --> 00:02:38,652
[SPEAKER_00]: He understands how un-arisen doubt arises and how arisen doubt is abandoned.

27
00:02:39,252 --> 00:02:41,894
[SPEAKER_00]: And how abandoned doubt does not arise again in the future.

28
00:02:43,095 --> 00:02:44,377
[SPEAKER_00]: That is called purity of thought.

29
00:02:44,817 --> 00:02:49,821
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the Buddha finished with the verses saying these are these... there are these three kinds of purity.

30
00:02:50,442 --> 00:02:53,405
[SPEAKER_00]: Purity of body, purity of speech and purity of thought.

31
00:02:54,439 --> 00:02:58,140
[SPEAKER_00]: One who is clean and down with purity has washed off all evil they say.

32
00:03:01,582 --> 00:03:09,224
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, as we can see, obviously, the sutta addresses the three fields of action.

33
00:03:10,865 --> 00:03:13,826
[SPEAKER_00]: Action by body, by speech and thought.

34
00:03:15,526 --> 00:03:21,369
[SPEAKER_00]: So, purity of body, self-explanatory, the precepts, the virtue, that's obviously

35
00:03:23,147 --> 00:03:28,830
[SPEAKER_00]: Summed up in the in the three major ones, which is killing, stealing and insulipacy.

36
00:03:29,911 --> 00:03:40,217
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, it includes the domain of speech, which is anything from false speech, malicious, harsh, pointless, trivial, useless.

37
00:03:40,297 --> 00:03:41,978
[SPEAKER_00]: That's avoiding those things.

38
00:03:42,038 --> 00:03:43,439
[SPEAKER_00]: It's how one purifies the speech.

39
00:03:43,739 --> 00:03:46,241
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, the puritive thought in this sutta was

40
00:03:48,733 --> 00:03:54,687
[SPEAKER_00]: expressed through, basically, understanding, full understanding, of the five hindrances.

41
00:03:56,344 --> 00:04:13,917
[SPEAKER_00]: So, understanding of central desire, understanding the mind affected with central desire, when the mind is affected with central desire, understanding the mind unaffected by central desire, when the mind is not affected by it, understanding how it arises, understanding how it's abandoned, and understanding how it doesn't arise.

42
00:04:14,537 --> 00:04:23,684
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and then the same goes for real will, indolence and lethargy, restlessness and anxiety, and then doubt as well.

43
00:04:24,825 --> 00:04:25,085
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

44
00:04:26,378 --> 00:04:41,531
[SPEAKER_00]: The interesting thing, and I think Venerable Nigam mentioned that on our Sutta website, is to note that how the Sutta does not define purity by the absence of the five hindrances.

45
00:04:41,571 --> 00:04:47,776
[SPEAKER_00]: It defines it by knowing that they are present when they are present, and knowing that they are absent when they are absent.

46
00:04:47,916 --> 00:04:50,518
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that is the purity of thought.

47
00:04:50,658 --> 00:04:53,921
[SPEAKER_00]: Purity of thought is not not having the mind affected with sensuality.

48
00:04:55,681 --> 00:05:05,806
[SPEAKER_00]: The purity of thought is basically knowing the mind affected with sensuality as mind affected with sensuality, mind affected with doubt or ill-will as mind affected with those things.

49
00:05:07,607 --> 00:05:10,188
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like...

50
00:05:12,069 --> 00:05:24,935
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is obviously that presence of those things, the hindrances, presence of sensuality, ill-will, sloth and torpor, restlessness and anxiety and doubt,

51
00:05:26,065 --> 00:05:28,727
[SPEAKER_00]: They're not in and of themselves.

52
00:05:29,327 --> 00:05:30,368
[SPEAKER_00]: They're not hindrances.

53
00:05:31,689 --> 00:05:38,074
[SPEAKER_00]: It's... when they're present in the mind, when that is not known, that is how one is hindered.

54
00:05:40,495 --> 00:05:44,078
[SPEAKER_00]: When it's known, one is unhindered, pretty much, by the same thing.

55
00:05:44,959 --> 00:05:53,708
[SPEAKER_00]: There was that other sutta, I think we might have done it even during these sessions, where it states exactly the same.

56
00:05:53,788 --> 00:06:00,254
[SPEAKER_00]: It says there are things, they're like signs of beauty that come and mind picks up on them.

57
00:06:01,356 --> 00:06:05,179
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's like that in and of itself does not constitute a hindrance.

58
00:06:05,921 --> 00:06:18,799
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's when the mind, or rather when you attend wrongly to that experience of mind affected or mind seeing a sign of beauty, attending wrongly, ayoniso manasikāra, is how?

59
00:06:20,214 --> 00:06:21,294
[SPEAKER_00]: You become hindered by it.

60
00:06:21,495 --> 00:06:25,856
[SPEAKER_00]: It's how that sensuality becomes something you're affected.

61
00:06:26,317 --> 00:06:35,401
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, say, there is a sign of ugly, sign of kind of aversion, which in and of itself, again, does not constitute a hindrance.

62
00:06:35,481 --> 00:06:38,082
[SPEAKER_00]: Attending to it wrongly, you are hindered.

63
00:06:38,362 --> 00:06:42,564
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, not seeing the mind, not seeing the wild animal,

64
00:06:43,701 --> 00:06:54,426
[SPEAKER_00]: To the sufficient extent, as something that's percipient or something that's, say, under the pressure of sign of beauty or sign of aversion, that's how you become hindered.

65
00:06:54,466 --> 00:07:07,893
[SPEAKER_00]: And then he went to explain the same for these other five hindrances, saying, pretty much, like, being lazy, acting lazily after the meal and so on, is how you become hindered by indolence and lethargy, wrongly attending to that.

66
00:07:09,434 --> 00:07:10,635
[SPEAKER_00]: Restlessness and anxiety.

67
00:07:11,075 --> 00:07:12,216
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, was it the doubt?

68
00:07:12,256 --> 00:07:15,719
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, the doubt says there are things that are... that lend themselves to doubt.

69
00:07:16,480 --> 00:07:21,805
[SPEAKER_00]: But, again, still does not... do not constitute a hindrance of doubt.

70
00:07:22,826 --> 00:07:32,855
[SPEAKER_00]: Only wrongly attending to the mind that's under the pressure of things that are ambiguous in the nature, is how you fall into the hindrance of doubt.

71
00:07:33,792 --> 00:07:43,597
[SPEAKER_00]: So, learning how to attend rightly does not remove the whole picture, does not prevent the sign of beauty, or sign of ugly, or thing suitable to doubt, or lack of energy.

72
00:07:44,217 --> 00:07:50,580
[SPEAKER_00]: But it prevents you falling into the framework of that hindrance, basically.

73
00:07:50,660 --> 00:07:52,361
[SPEAKER_00]: It prevents you being trapped by it.

74
00:07:53,842 --> 00:07:56,423
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what Nigga pointed out.

75
00:07:56,443 --> 00:07:58,384
[SPEAKER_00]: There are hindrances when there is craving.

76
00:07:59,186 --> 00:08:16,537
[SPEAKER_00]: And without ignorance, craving cannot exist, which means, basically, if you are rightly attending, or seeing the mind clearly to the right extent, as something that's under the pressure of, say, sign of beauty, you won't be affected amidst those things.

77
00:08:17,158 --> 00:08:21,120
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you are unhindered amidst the world of hindrances, pretty much.

78
00:08:22,501 --> 00:08:28,305
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and that's... he gave a... he gave an analogy in his notes, saying, it's like a... if someone has the key,

79
00:08:29,372 --> 00:08:34,754
[SPEAKER_00]: For a cell door, and knows for a fact, they can get out at will, whenever they want.

80
00:08:36,454 --> 00:08:38,895
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you really call that an imprisonment?

81
00:08:39,435 --> 00:08:54,580
[SPEAKER_00]: If nobody else knows about a key, only you, only you have it, even people that think you are trapped in, don't know about it, and you could, at will, just open the door and leave, would it be correct to say you are the one who is imprisoned?

82
00:08:55,561 --> 00:08:55,681
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

83
00:08:56,341 --> 00:08:57,001
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the point.

84
00:08:58,629 --> 00:09:06,871
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, so the sutta is short, but I did kind of extrapolate a few practical questions.

85
00:09:08,963 --> 00:09:09,863
[SPEAKER_00]: on the basis of it.

86
00:09:10,624 --> 00:09:19,132
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, obviously, you know, we speak about purity of body, purity of virtue often, so we're not going to dwell on that too much.

87
00:09:20,753 --> 00:09:23,595
[SPEAKER_00]: You can listen to the previous talks for that.

88
00:09:23,816 --> 00:09:30,461
[SPEAKER_00]: But purity of speech and then, obviously, purity of thought is the most refined and it's not just about

89
00:09:31,462 --> 00:09:40,844
[SPEAKER_00]: Say, avoiding thinking about certain things and so on, but it's measured by the clarity of the understanding of the animal, of the citta.

90
00:09:42,064 --> 00:09:59,027
[SPEAKER_00]: So, to what extent... Say, when the sensuality arises, or ill-will, to what extent do you regard that experience as a whole, as mind affected with ill-will that I'm experiencing, or

91
00:10:00,456 --> 00:10:10,806
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm affected by... affected by ill-will through basically experiencing this or that that upset me in the world and mind is not even seen in that equation.

92
00:10:12,107 --> 00:10:25,279
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how much of it do you see like it's between you and the mind or how much of it do you see out between me and whatever circumstance in the world made me upset or made me lustful or made me doubtful?

93
00:10:26,093 --> 00:10:26,854
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's the point.

94
00:10:27,634 --> 00:10:29,996
[SPEAKER_00]: You begin by not seeing the mind at all.

95
00:10:30,036 --> 00:10:32,417
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's it's it's it's all there in the world.

96
00:10:32,477 --> 00:10:36,720
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all on the level of sense objects and so on.

97
00:10:36,740 --> 00:10:55,333
[SPEAKER_00]: Through containers, through containers of virtue, purifying your speech, right choices and everything else we often talk about, you are, as I said many times before, creating some... a degree of some space in between where you could actually start to notice your mind as a thing in between there.

98
00:10:56,860 --> 00:11:01,742
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you might initially, like, oh, this upset me, or that made me full of desire.

99
00:11:02,362 --> 00:11:09,524
[SPEAKER_00]: But, before that, or rather between you and what you're seeing, there is that animal sitting there, affected by that thing.

100
00:11:10,544 --> 00:11:11,885
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what needs to be seen.

101
00:11:13,145 --> 00:11:15,506
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, fundamentally, that's really the root of the problem.

102
00:11:16,246 --> 00:11:17,606
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you practice restraint.

103
00:11:17,907 --> 00:11:22,368
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you pay attention to the choice of your actions.

104
00:11:24,197 --> 00:11:29,520
[SPEAKER_00]: Because all of that feeds that animal that's initially invisible to you, that mind.

105
00:11:31,981 --> 00:11:44,247
[SPEAKER_00]: So the purity of thought is measured and trained through noticing that state of the animal, not trying to get rid of it, not trying to change it, just containing it.

106
00:11:45,467 --> 00:11:50,390
[SPEAKER_00]: When I say do things on the right level, it really just means contain them to the right extent.

107
00:11:52,800 --> 00:11:59,447
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you're too preoccupied of trying to resolve something instantaneously, well, you don't want to be containing it.

108
00:12:03,291 --> 00:12:08,877
[SPEAKER_00]: You're afraid that it might spill out and so on, which means, okay, you haven't developed the container sufficiently enough.

109
00:12:10,199 --> 00:12:13,102
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why it's just... it's not even like... there is

110
00:12:14,313 --> 00:12:15,533
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no other way, really.

111
00:12:15,553 --> 00:12:17,534
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you can't bypass this.

112
00:12:17,634 --> 00:12:34,399
[SPEAKER_00]: The only way to understand the mind to the full extent as something affected by a hindrance, and therefore you become unaffected by it, is to contain it to the extent necessary, to endure it to the extent necessary until that container is developed sufficiently enough.

113
00:12:35,319 --> 00:12:36,139
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no other way.

114
00:12:37,439 --> 00:12:39,500
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how much of that endurance and container

115
00:12:41,870 --> 00:12:47,952
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether it's going to be unpleasant, or not so unpleasant, depends on how much you're really resisting the idea of it, or not.

116
00:12:52,073 --> 00:12:56,895
[SPEAKER_00]: So, eh... Let me go back to this one.

117
00:12:59,896 --> 00:13:06,918
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, the questions for reflection, that I, as I said, extrapolated.

118
00:13:08,606 --> 00:13:16,371
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, for example, and again, these things can be, you know, where if you say, okay, I don't see my mind, therefore this sutta doesn't apply to me.

119
00:13:16,391 --> 00:13:23,795
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, fundamentally, sure, you need to see your mind for this to make any sense, but that doesn't mean you shouldn't be trying, as I said there before.

120
00:13:25,096 --> 00:13:35,742
[SPEAKER_00]: So, meditation, samādhi, any form of, say, composure, should be revolving

121
00:13:37,165 --> 00:13:42,007
[SPEAKER_00]: And be preoccupied, really, with learning how to contain that mind.

122
00:13:42,147 --> 00:14:00,135
[SPEAKER_00]: How to not, through ayoniso manasikāra, you yourself, spill out and blame the world, blame the circumstances, blame the sign of beauty, blame the sign of ugly, blame things suitable that lend themselves to doubt easily, as that which is doubt, as that which is ill-will.

123
00:14:00,915 --> 00:14:02,036
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what samādhi is.

124
00:14:03,014 --> 00:14:07,737
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not about removing the whole picture and not experiencing any of those disturbing things.

125
00:14:07,837 --> 00:14:10,940
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about training in their containment.

126
00:14:12,301 --> 00:14:21,267
[SPEAKER_00]: So, any form of meditation should really be a informed reflection, to call it that way.

127
00:14:21,567 --> 00:14:32,435
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can ask yourself, like, in regard to this sutta in particular, you could ask yourself, so, okay, how, like, my recent actions,

128
00:14:33,602 --> 00:14:41,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Today, two days ago, I don't know, last week, as far as I can recently remember, how often were they driven by desire?

129
00:14:43,027 --> 00:14:44,047
[SPEAKER_00]: Carelessness and ill-will.

130
00:14:44,348 --> 00:14:54,473
[SPEAKER_00]: And that already implies how often did I look past the state of mind and acted towards the world for pleasure, avoiding pain, resolving doubt,

131
00:14:55,732 --> 00:14:59,254
[SPEAKER_00]: Which implicitly, obviously, assumes that that's where the problem was.

132
00:15:00,075 --> 00:15:19,208
[SPEAKER_00]: And even if I theoretically learned that that's not where the problem was, how often did I give in to my still ingrained habit of still acting out towards the domain of the world through body, speech and mind, even if... even despite theoretically knowing that that's not where I should be looking for solution.

133
00:15:19,328 --> 00:15:21,530
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how often did I allow myself to act out?

134
00:15:24,973 --> 00:15:30,155
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, obviously, if you thought, okay, I could have done better, maybe I did good, maybe I didn't.

135
00:15:30,995 --> 00:15:37,918
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is, okay, it's not going to be perfect, but the point is to start to recognize, okay, how many times I didn't.

136
00:15:37,978 --> 00:15:42,019
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how many times I actually didn't act out despite the pressure.

137
00:15:43,320 --> 00:15:49,562
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's what this... why this sutta is important, because it tells you, see, that's already a level of purity.

138
00:15:51,362 --> 00:15:59,673
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, the initial view and attitude by most people would be complete absence of these hindrances is what purity is.

139
00:15:59,773 --> 00:16:09,025
[SPEAKER_00]: Therefore, they'd fall into these self-hypnotic meditation techniques, not incidentally, because that's the layer when you are unaware of pretty much everything.

140
00:16:10,738 --> 00:16:13,259
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you feel like this is the purest because there is nothing there.

141
00:16:13,519 --> 00:16:16,460
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, there's a lot there, obviously, it's just that you're not noticing it.

142
00:16:17,160 --> 00:16:21,081
[SPEAKER_00]: But the point is, the purity is not measured by the absence.

143
00:16:21,782 --> 00:16:31,965
[SPEAKER_00]: The purity is measured by your clarity in regard to whatever's present of those disturbing, five disturbing things.

144
00:16:31,985 --> 00:16:33,986
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you ask yourself, how often did I act out?

145
00:16:35,710 --> 00:16:39,572
[SPEAKER_00]: This many times, that, in this instance, that instance... How often I didn't?

146
00:16:40,292 --> 00:16:41,712
[SPEAKER_00]: How often did I restrain myself?

147
00:16:42,793 --> 00:16:51,456
[SPEAKER_00]: And the point is, as I said, to rewrite that expectation that the absence of those things all together is the benchmark.

148
00:16:51,696 --> 00:16:52,117
[SPEAKER_00]: It isn't.

149
00:16:53,257 --> 00:16:56,718
[SPEAKER_00]: But to start to develop the clarity in regard to the difference as well.

150
00:16:56,879 --> 00:17:00,100
[SPEAKER_00]: The difference between, well, how was it when I acted out?

151
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:02,741
[SPEAKER_00]: When I gave in to my old assumptions?

152
00:17:03,621 --> 00:17:05,202
[SPEAKER_00]: And how did it feel when I didn't?

153
00:17:06,023 --> 00:17:07,604
[SPEAKER_00]: What's the difference between the two?

154
00:17:08,784 --> 00:17:17,748
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what's the difference between an enduring pressure to act out and then act out and enduring pressure to act out and not acting out?

155
00:17:20,938 --> 00:17:32,782
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the reason people choose acting out perpetually, repeatedly, is because not acting out, at the time, feels like, oh, unbearable.

156
00:17:33,103 --> 00:17:33,803
[SPEAKER_00]: I have to act out.

157
00:17:34,083 --> 00:17:34,963
[SPEAKER_00]: I just have to act out.

158
00:17:35,183 --> 00:17:36,084
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll deal with it later.

159
00:17:36,204 --> 00:17:36,984
[SPEAKER_00]: I have to act out.

160
00:17:38,124 --> 00:17:47,388
[SPEAKER_00]: But the more familiar you become with it, even in, like, through this kind of retroactive contemplation of, hmm, how did it feel when I actually restrained myself,

161
00:17:48,390 --> 00:17:49,490
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you feel when I didn't?

162
00:17:50,111 --> 00:17:52,031
[SPEAKER_00]: What was the difference?

163
00:17:52,451 --> 00:17:56,653
[SPEAKER_00]: Even that can be gradually familiarizing you with it.

164
00:17:57,073 --> 00:18:03,295
[SPEAKER_00]: And you actually get to understand and kind of develop a broader view that, well, it wasn't that bad.

165
00:18:03,615 --> 00:18:05,315
[SPEAKER_00]: It feels that bad at the moment.

166
00:18:05,395 --> 00:18:10,857
[SPEAKER_00]: But from a point of view of a bigger picture, I actually contained it.

167
00:18:12,103 --> 00:18:12,984
[SPEAKER_00]: And it wasn't too bad.

168
00:18:13,684 --> 00:18:20,108
[SPEAKER_00]: So, next time, you can remind... well, you would be automatically reminded of that, if you frequently recollect those things.

169
00:18:20,929 --> 00:18:24,271
[SPEAKER_00]: Next time, when it feels like, oh, I can't bear this, I have to act out.

170
00:18:24,471 --> 00:18:26,433
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's like, but I already...

171
00:18:26,873 --> 00:18:28,374
[SPEAKER_00]: I bought it in the past.

172
00:18:28,414 --> 00:18:31,376
[SPEAKER_00]: It wasn't...

173
00:18:31,716 --> 00:18:32,937
[SPEAKER_00]: It was an end of the world, for sure.

174
00:18:33,177 --> 00:18:34,518
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why don't I do it again?

175
00:18:36,068 --> 00:18:43,393
[SPEAKER_00]: And it kind of re-inform your own context at the time when that pressure is really, like, trying to make you act.

176
00:18:44,494 --> 00:18:57,983
[SPEAKER_00]: So, frequently recollecting that, reminding yourself, and, as I said, trying to discern the difference, even if it's like a vague, oh, it felt like this, felt like that, you don't need to be, like, like, you know, super psychologically accurate or something.

177
00:18:58,043 --> 00:19:04,327
[SPEAKER_00]: It's more like just being familiar with the other side of things, such as pressure came up and I didn't act.

178
00:19:07,284 --> 00:19:17,507
[SPEAKER_00]: First, you learn to not, again, not overshoot the mark, which is, pressure came up, I didn't act, but I feel like I must still get rid of the pressure, because that's what the purity is.

179
00:19:17,567 --> 00:19:18,267
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's not.

180
00:19:18,327 --> 00:19:19,168
[SPEAKER_00]: You learn that it isn't.

181
00:19:19,248 --> 00:19:24,109
[SPEAKER_00]: The purity is simply maintaining the clear picture, regardless of what state of the wild animal is.

182
00:19:25,289 --> 00:19:27,870
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the animal can be kicking, screaming, trying to bite you.

183
00:19:27,890 --> 00:19:33,972
[SPEAKER_00]: You realize, that's fine, as long as I don't give in to this and don't act out of it.

184
00:19:36,249 --> 00:19:38,089
[SPEAKER_00]: It will have to calm down, basically.

185
00:19:38,150 --> 00:19:39,070
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what you understand.

186
00:19:39,510 --> 00:19:44,451
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's where that patient, the patient's khanti aspect comes from.

187
00:19:45,151 --> 00:19:54,373
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, like, another, say, retroactive reflection that you can do, would be in regard to the speech.

188
00:19:54,993 --> 00:19:58,374
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's the same principle, obviously, but now you're refining it.

189
00:19:59,514 --> 00:20:01,894
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, you can say, yeah, I didn't act.

190
00:20:02,095 --> 00:20:03,835
[SPEAKER_00]: I wanted really to do this.

191
00:20:05,395 --> 00:20:06,855
[SPEAKER_00]: I tried to act out my body, but I didn't.

192
00:20:07,195 --> 00:20:09,316
[SPEAKER_00]: However, you know, I did say something.

193
00:20:11,276 --> 00:20:12,936
[SPEAKER_00]: It wasn't too bad, but it couldn't be better.

194
00:20:12,996 --> 00:20:16,537
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now you are addressing that domain, the domain of speech.

195
00:20:16,737 --> 00:20:23,898
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, you can do that as a daily practice, not out of some sort of neurosis, trying to find out the answers.

196
00:20:25,018 --> 00:20:28,119
[SPEAKER_00]: They will then magically make the whole thing fall into place.

197
00:20:28,219 --> 00:20:29,039
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's more like, no, no.

198
00:20:29,619 --> 00:20:33,540
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm going through the motions of actively reminding myself

199
00:20:34,514 --> 00:20:37,575
[SPEAKER_00]: of what I chose to do and what I didn't.

200
00:20:37,935 --> 00:20:40,236
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether I chose restraint or giving in.

201
00:20:42,236 --> 00:20:47,298
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether I spoke out of greed, out of aversion, out of irritation, out of hindrance, or I didn't.

202
00:20:50,939 --> 00:20:56,581
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not about learning something new, in that sense, like, as I said, digging up for new revelations, information.

203
00:20:56,601 --> 00:20:59,682
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about just reminding the mind

204
00:21:01,281 --> 00:21:05,602
[SPEAKER_00]: Of what it already knows, but it kind of tends to look past it.

205
00:21:08,043 --> 00:21:08,864
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not...

206
00:21:09,424 --> 00:21:23,088
[SPEAKER_00]: These reflections, basically, if you're doing them for that purpose, it might take some time, as I said, to get used to it, but if you're doing it for that purpose, just this type of questioning, it's already forming a type of samādhi.

207
00:21:23,289 --> 00:21:26,390
[SPEAKER_00]: It's already containing your mind rightly.

208
00:21:26,430 --> 00:21:26,930
[SPEAKER_00]: It's already

209
00:21:27,899 --> 00:21:38,303
[SPEAKER_00]: Increasing, however, you know, whatever, like, to whatever minuscule degree, it's already... but it's still increasing a some degree of clarity in regard to the whole picture.

210
00:21:41,384 --> 00:21:53,129
[SPEAKER_00]: So, recollect, again, about your recent speech, about today, about yesterday, and you say, oh, I said something I shouldn't have.

211
00:21:53,269 --> 00:21:56,570
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, you realize, okay, now it's not about resolving that.

212
00:21:58,565 --> 00:21:59,966
[SPEAKER_00]: Making grand determinations.

213
00:22:00,026 --> 00:22:03,249
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll never do this, and I'll never do that, and I'll never go there again, because like...

214
00:22:03,669 --> 00:22:05,130
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like all of that secondary.

215
00:22:05,991 --> 00:22:16,940
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about familiarizing yourself with the state of mind, with your current experience, that has recollected the fact that mind got the better of you, made you act.

216
00:22:17,781 --> 00:22:20,463
[SPEAKER_00]: How does it feel now, when you shed light to that?

217
00:22:20,583 --> 00:22:21,484
[SPEAKER_00]: It's gonna feel unpleasant.

218
00:22:21,884 --> 00:22:22,625
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the point.

219
00:22:25,568 --> 00:22:29,750
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, you might have a tendency, oh, I'm worthless and I'll have more... No, no, no.

220
00:22:29,830 --> 00:22:31,010
[SPEAKER_00]: Now that's going too far.

221
00:22:31,350 --> 00:22:32,651
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, question that now.

222
00:22:35,852 --> 00:22:37,413
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's about familiarization.

223
00:22:37,533 --> 00:22:41,895
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether it's affecting the mind pleasantly, unpleasantly, that's not the point.

224
00:22:42,615 --> 00:22:44,976
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the trap you should try and not fall into.

225
00:22:46,316 --> 00:22:48,117
[SPEAKER_00]: Be concerned about how it feels.

226
00:22:49,598 --> 00:22:51,178
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, you're containing it.

227
00:22:54,580 --> 00:22:54,900
[SPEAKER_00]: And then,

228
00:22:56,793 --> 00:23:01,095
[SPEAKER_00]: Extend the same reflection further, in the sense of like, eh...

229
00:23:01,115 --> 00:23:04,277
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, I did well or I didn't do well.

230
00:23:04,597 --> 00:23:06,458
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, this is how it feels.

231
00:23:06,598 --> 00:23:08,139
[SPEAKER_00]: This is how the difference feels.

232
00:23:09,260 --> 00:23:16,344
[SPEAKER_00]: Let me remind myself of the values I should try and then, eh... persist through, eh... tomorrow and next day and the day after.

233
00:23:17,624 --> 00:23:22,167
[SPEAKER_00]: If the speech is beneficial, timely, necessary, if I'm unsure about it,

234
00:23:22,988 --> 00:23:32,831
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, then I'll probably just veer on the side of restraint, but either way, remind yourself about choosing to speak, basically.

235
00:23:32,931 --> 00:23:43,355
[SPEAKER_00]: And when the pressure to say something builds up, again, you don't have to just every single time say no to it, but you need to have perspective in regard to it, regardless of how it feels.

236
00:23:46,754 --> 00:23:53,440
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that kind of brings me to the third point that you can be asking yourself and thinking about, which is...

237
00:23:54,241 --> 00:24:04,509
[SPEAKER_00]: So, do you clearly or how clearly do you recognize the context behind any currently enduring pressure, any hindrance, while it is present?

238
00:24:05,610 --> 00:24:13,877
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, when you are acutely pressured to act out, towards sensuality or out of ill-will and so on,

239
00:24:14,868 --> 00:24:19,275
[SPEAKER_00]: How much of the context do you still retain, or are able to retain?

240
00:24:21,813 --> 00:24:22,633
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it like none?

241
00:24:22,773 --> 00:24:27,215
[SPEAKER_00]: You only see it afterwards or you are able to keep some perspective?

242
00:24:28,015 --> 00:24:35,198
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, obviously, that's kind of something that should be, again, revisited, recollected, reminded and developed.

243
00:24:35,678 --> 00:24:36,979
[SPEAKER_00]: And how do you develop that context?

244
00:24:37,019 --> 00:24:40,280
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, exactly how I just described the first two reflections.

245
00:24:40,560 --> 00:24:41,520
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you develop it.

246
00:24:41,560 --> 00:24:45,542
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how, through this frequent reminding,

247
00:24:46,524 --> 00:24:53,547
[SPEAKER_00]: Of what's skillful, what's unskillful, the difference between acting out, the difference between not acting out and staying with the pressure.

248
00:24:54,327 --> 00:24:57,348
[SPEAKER_00]: That is how you are, as I said, informing that context.

249
00:24:57,448 --> 00:25:09,052
[SPEAKER_00]: And the clearer you become regarding that experience as a whole, then the acuteness of the hindrance will be diminished to that extent.

250
00:25:09,112 --> 00:25:15,835
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, hindrance can be, like, really almost overwhelming you, but the context remains to not act out,

251
00:25:16,678 --> 00:25:22,983
[SPEAKER_00]: That it's the state of the mind that needs to be contained, not be projected onto the world, towards the sense objects, and then fall into a trap.

252
00:25:23,744 --> 00:25:31,230
[SPEAKER_00]: So, through that gradual, as I said, broadening of that context, is how you eventually will stop acting out.

253
00:25:32,070 --> 00:25:43,880
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, like, obviously, you abandon course acting outs, but these, these, eh... finer ways, in terms of, like, the speech and so on, where even your intention might be ambiguous and stuff, well, that's how you get to uproot it.

254
00:25:44,623 --> 00:26:04,530
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you can't necessarily pinpoint as easily as these coarse forms of actions, and you don't have to, because if the context becomes imperturbable enough, that endurance is the way out, not acting out as the way out, anything within that will have to... will have to eventually completely be eradicated.

255
00:26:04,870 --> 00:26:05,070
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

256
00:26:07,351 --> 00:26:11,332
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, eh... Yeah, another thing you can be asking yourself,

257
00:26:12,483 --> 00:26:29,512
[SPEAKER_00]: Following the same line of the five hindrances is, say, when you are not acutely pressured, do you recognize certain, say, environmental triggers, conditions, that would act as precursor to a hindrance, precursor to a pressure?

258
00:26:30,632 --> 00:26:38,716
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said, obviously, they're going to be the obvious ones, which presumably you would have been avoiding well beforehand, but even the subtler ones.

259
00:26:40,237 --> 00:26:41,558
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, can you foresee

260
00:26:43,138 --> 00:26:52,765
[SPEAKER_00]: Where certain experiences, signs and features of those experiences are leading to, before it's too late, before the pressure is too much and you can't stop now.

261
00:26:53,726 --> 00:26:55,267
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's obviously important.

262
00:26:55,287 --> 00:27:09,537
[SPEAKER_00]: That's like... that's the value of like learning things beforehand, because if you don't start anticipating where a certain set of choices, actions and so on is taking you,

263
00:27:10,382 --> 00:27:13,443
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, there will be no mind development for you, really.

264
00:27:14,164 --> 00:27:17,185
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's that simile of the cook that the Buddha talked about.

265
00:27:20,526 --> 00:27:37,453
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the last one, which, yeah, it's kind of useful and ties in with what I already said, which is, can you recall an instance when a hindrance actually ceased on its own?

266
00:27:38,534 --> 00:27:39,334
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, completely.

267
00:27:41,263 --> 00:27:49,330
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you didn't act out, you endured it, you contained it, and then, next thing you know, it's... it was gone.

268
00:27:51,011 --> 00:28:05,524
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, it's not because you're expecting to learn something new from it, but it's in order to recollect it, in order to bring it back, because there will be subtler aspects of that the mind is simply overlooking.

269
00:28:06,204 --> 00:28:09,427
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's to become more and more familiar with how did that feel.

270
00:28:11,365 --> 00:28:22,471
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, see, whenever there is an acute hindrance pressuring you to act out, towards desire, towards this, towards that, staying with the pressure feels like it has no end.

271
00:28:23,171 --> 00:28:31,676
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can say no to your desire once or twice, but the pressure is still there, the pressure is still there, and basically the mind... well, you fall into this view,

272
00:28:33,279 --> 00:28:42,026
[SPEAKER_00]: There and then, that, well, if you don't act out, this will be kind of trapping you and closing you this pressure forever.

273
00:28:43,187 --> 00:28:46,630
[SPEAKER_00]: All that you know, like, and you can tell to yourself, no, of course, nothing lasts forever.

274
00:28:46,650 --> 00:28:47,310
[SPEAKER_00]: I know it will stop.

275
00:28:47,711 --> 00:28:48,291
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter.

276
00:28:48,791 --> 00:28:50,613
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind doesn't listen to that.

277
00:28:50,753 --> 00:28:58,239
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're telling to yourself, oh, everything will pass away and impermanence, but the mind doesn't understand that, doesn't hear it.

278
00:28:59,113 --> 00:28:59,613
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is that?

279
00:28:59,913 --> 00:29:17,762
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, as I said many times before, because it doesn't speak your language, the practical reason for the mind not really taking any heed of the fact that, yes, the pressure will have to cease, and you know it, yet the mind still acts like it will never cease, it's because, well, you haven't been reflecting on it long enough.

280
00:29:18,480 --> 00:29:29,551
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you haven't been going through the repetition of those recollections sufficiently enough for it to sink in, for it, for the mind to really become convinced.

281
00:29:29,771 --> 00:29:32,074
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, yeah, of course, yeah, no, it will cease.

282
00:29:33,397 --> 00:29:44,323
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why you should be also, yeah, recollect the hindrance, recollect the triggers and so on, but also recollect the times when actually you didn't act out and the hindrance subsided on its own.

283
00:29:44,683 --> 00:29:45,624
[SPEAKER_00]: The pressure was gone.

284
00:29:46,004 --> 00:29:49,105
[SPEAKER_00]: You didn't act out, you didn't do anything on account of it and it was gone.

285
00:29:49,526 --> 00:29:50,526
[SPEAKER_00]: How did that feel?

286
00:29:53,788 --> 00:29:56,890
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, ask the question, don't necessarily seek the answer.

287
00:29:57,810 --> 00:29:59,031
[SPEAKER_00]: Because asking the question

288
00:30:00,538 --> 00:30:01,339
[SPEAKER_00]: Reveals the mind.

289
00:30:01,639 --> 00:30:03,620
[SPEAKER_00]: Or leaves the mind with it, so to speak.

290
00:30:03,700 --> 00:30:06,362
[SPEAKER_00]: And the mind will remember how it felt.

291
00:30:06,562 --> 00:30:08,263
[SPEAKER_00]: The animal will remember how it felt.

292
00:30:09,144 --> 00:30:11,945
[SPEAKER_00]: That's really what you... whom you're asking for.

293
00:30:12,025 --> 00:30:13,126
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not asking for yourself.

294
00:30:13,847 --> 00:30:14,287
[SPEAKER_00]: How did it feel?

295
00:30:14,307 --> 00:30:14,967
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it felt...

296
00:30:15,268 --> 00:30:15,868
[SPEAKER_00]: It's irrelevant.

297
00:30:16,268 --> 00:30:17,149
[SPEAKER_00]: How did it feel?

298
00:30:17,549 --> 00:30:18,870
[SPEAKER_00]: And feel how it felt.

299
00:30:19,770 --> 00:30:21,712
[SPEAKER_00]: Get the animal to feel it how it felt.

300
00:30:23,993 --> 00:30:28,116
[SPEAKER_00]: So, next time, the acute pressure is there, trying to overwhelm you.

301
00:30:29,412 --> 00:30:46,286
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, you might ask the question, but you might not even have to ask the question, because that is the exact instance that you've been recalling, recollecting, so the mind will now be reminded of what you reminded it beforehand, which is how it felt when the thing ceased, which means, oh, it will cease.

302
00:30:47,387 --> 00:30:48,568
[SPEAKER_00]: And now the animal got it.

303
00:30:49,638 --> 00:30:50,920
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, you can forget again.

304
00:30:50,960 --> 00:30:52,943
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's not like you do it once and you're done.

305
00:30:53,704 --> 00:30:57,810
[SPEAKER_00]: But the point is to get the animal to feel the difference.

306
00:30:58,470 --> 00:30:59,692
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it knows the difference.

307
00:30:59,892 --> 00:31:01,234
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, even if somebody

308
00:31:02,348 --> 00:31:03,949
[SPEAKER_00]: Made zero effort in practice.

309
00:31:04,530 --> 00:31:19,682
[SPEAKER_00]: Simply by by going through experiences on a daily basis, there must have been an instance where there was an acute sensuality or ill will and for one reason or the other, a sutta or a puthujjana, knowing nothing of the dhamma, for whatever reason, was not able to act out.

310
00:31:19,902 --> 00:31:22,805
[SPEAKER_00]: Was just left with that pent-up pressure.

311
00:31:24,046 --> 00:31:26,248
[SPEAKER_00]: Couldn't act out, couldn't do anything about it.

312
00:31:27,718 --> 00:31:28,438
[SPEAKER_00]: And it disappeared.

313
00:31:28,919 --> 00:31:37,803
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just that, you know, again, if if he's completely uninstructed, he will not know to recollect that, the difference, and start start highlighting the difference.

314
00:31:37,843 --> 00:31:44,066
[SPEAKER_00]: He'll just look past it, and move on, and continue acting towards objects in the world as if they were the problem.

315
00:31:45,666 --> 00:31:57,152
[SPEAKER_00]: But now you know better, so you want to recollect how it felt, to develop a context that will, regardless of how powerful the pressure might be,

316
00:31:58,552 --> 00:32:07,297
[SPEAKER_00]: ceases... ceases immediately, like, eh... instantane... or, simultaneously, ce... eh... recognizes that it's end.

317
00:32:08,978 --> 00:32:18,403
[SPEAKER_00]: It recognizes that doesn't matter how long this lasts, how acute it might get, I remember how it ends.

318
00:32:19,944 --> 00:32:27,568
[SPEAKER_00]: Because every hindrance ends the same, through that feeling of it's gone, relieved, unhindered, no weight,

319
00:32:29,184 --> 00:32:38,613
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that will be sufficient to remind the animal to not panic, not feel as enclosed and entrapped as it otherwise would.

320
00:32:40,395 --> 00:32:41,476
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why that's important.

321
00:32:41,956 --> 00:32:44,839
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why these reflections are the true meditations, really.

322
00:32:45,580 --> 00:32:52,566
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're asking these questions, not for the sake of, as I said, intellectual reasoning or finding some, like, psychological answers, but

323
00:32:53,498 --> 00:32:57,962
[SPEAKER_00]: Just to bring, shed the light and set up the context that are then left with the mind.

324
00:32:58,582 --> 00:33:00,364
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can ask a question and then sit with it.

325
00:33:00,904 --> 00:33:06,209
[SPEAKER_00]: And if the mind wanders away, come back, re-ask it or ask a different question in regards to similar.

326
00:33:06,269 --> 00:33:17,979
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the questioning part is what keeps you actively engaged, so you don't fall into the mindless mantra recitation or sensation watching method.

327
00:33:18,847 --> 00:33:20,728
[SPEAKER_00]: But you're not asking for the sake of answers, really.

328
00:33:21,429 --> 00:33:33,717
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the questioning part is what engages you, but then you stop halfway through for the mind to feel, to discern answers, for the answers to sink down to the mind.

329
00:33:33,977 --> 00:33:35,918
[SPEAKER_00]: Not for the sake of your theory.

330
00:33:37,880 --> 00:33:42,203
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's pretty much how you train in regard to purifying your thoughts.

331
00:33:44,924 --> 00:33:48,347
[SPEAKER_00]: Broadening the context on the basis of not acting out of hindrances,

332
00:33:49,313 --> 00:33:57,027
[SPEAKER_00]: Not trying to get rid of the pressure, containing it and finding the stable, untouched ground within that.

333
00:33:57,988 --> 00:33:58,810
[SPEAKER_00]: That's pretty much it.

